<p>Roosevelt made a short campaign stop in Mandan after arriving on the evening No. 5 train on October 4. He addressed a large crowd assembled at the depot. During the preceding days, the "Bull Moose" leader had also made speaking stops in Grand Forks, Fargo, Jamestown, and Bismarck. After the train stopped near the depot, Roosevelt appeared on the rear platform and began speaking about the new movement represented by the Progressive Party. He then spotted {% person "charlie-rowe" %}Charlie Rowe{% endperson %}, one of his ranch employees from the 1880s, and summoned him onto the platform. Roosevelt slapped him on the back with a "Hello, Charlie" and told the crowd about incidents from his ranching days in western North Dakota.</p>

<p>Twenty minutes later, the locomotive began to build steam for the next leg of the journey west. "Teddy" called out his goodbyes, saying that he was truly glad to be back in a region where a man was accepted for what he was worth and that he would always have a warm place in his heart for the sturdy manhood of the West. Roosevelt waved his hat to the crowd as the train slowly gathered speed for Dickinson.</p>

